are kept. request_line writes the composed request, and requested_ids reads its first DISPUTE findings line. The composed brief's findings-file line names the reference it carries; its first such line supplies the path, and no or empty line supplies none. parse reads RULING lines one physical line at a time and accepts only one FIX, SUCCESSOR, DISMISS, or ESCALATE ruling with a reason for every requested id; DISMISS additionally takes only out-of-scope, refuted, or not-worth-cost and a reason. Missing, duplicate, unrequested, malformed, or incomplete content refuses; no value is defaulted. SUCCESSOR takes a reason and no reason class; an unknown ruling refusal names FIX, SUCCESSOR, DISMISS, and ESCALATE in that order.\"\n rationale = \"Both adjudication routes need one grammar for the coordinator's findings input and the adjudicator's verdict. A single parser prevents a composed brief, entry point, and later loop sitting from accepting different finding sets or verdict meanings.\"\n test_refs = \"TC-334\"\n status = \"Approved\"\n@@ -3373,7 +3373,7 @@ sr_refs = [\"SR-234\"]\n title = \"Dispute brief assembly carries bounded findings to the adjudicator\"\n module = \"project-trajectory/scripts/adjudicate_brief.py\"\n code_symbol = \"DISPUTE/BRIEF_PROMPTS/dispute_values/_render_finding\"\n-detail = \"dispute_values accepts exactly one findings-file reference from an adjudication row, reads it through kitlib.dispute, and refuses an unreadable or malformed file. It accepts only a hexadecimal base..head range that git resolves, derives that range's commit and name-status facts, and fills the dispute template's range, commits, request, findings, and process-document values. _render_finding preserves each finding and held position between labelled delimiters. BRIEF_PROMPTS routes the dispute class to its shipped template, so the existing strict fill refuses a partial brief.\"\n+detail = \"dispute_values accepts exactly one findings-file reference from an adjudication row, reads it through kitlib.dispute, and refuses an unreadable or malformed file. It accepts only a hexadecimal base..head range that git resolves, derives that range's commit and name-status facts, and fills the dispute template's range, commits, request, findings, and process-document values. The request value carries, after the requested ids, the findings-file line naming the one reference the assembler read. _render_finding preserves each finding and held position between labelled delimiters. BRIEF_PROMPTS routes the dispute class to its shipped template, so the existing strict fill refuses a partial brief.\"\n rationale = \"The adjudicator needs the reviewer wording and the contested position as subjects to judge, while the range facts and review-scope link must be independently derived. Refusing an incomplete assembly avoids sending a judge a brief that appears complete but has lost a finding or its scope.\"\n test_refs = \"TC-334\"\n status = \"Approved\"\ndiff --git a/docs/runtime-flows.md b/docs/runtime-flows.md\nindex 27601cfab..b35b4d6b3 100644\n--- a/docs/runtime-flows.md\n+++ b/docs/runtime-flows.md\n@@ -261,7 +261,7 @@ sequenceDiagram\n     participant Agent as adjudicator session\n     participant Records as approved-row records\n     Coord->>Lane: confirm the judging lane claim (SR-231)\n-    Coord->>Verdict: reserve path and bind the judging brief and its judged rows' identities (LLR-306, LLR-359)\n+    Coord->>Verdict: reserve path and bind the judging brief, its judged rows' identities and a dispute brief's findings file (LLR-306, LLR-359, LLR-310)\n     Coord->>Agent: launch judging brief\n     Agent->>Verdict: commit verdict\n     Coord->>Verdict: validate and record accepted verdict\ndiff --git a/docs/test/test-cases.toml b/docs/test/test-cases.toml\nindex 31f775324..2eb07ce8a 100644\n--- a/docs/test/test-cases.toml\n+++ b/docs/test/test-cases.toml\n@@ -743,11 +743,11 @@ phase = 4\n [test.TC-069]\n verifies = [\"SR-155\", \"SR-236\", \"LLR-069\"]\n level = \"Integration\"\n-method = \"Run dual and single plan coverage, reference, graph, exclusion, review-verdict finding, dispute-ruling, SR/TC-diff, absent-registry, and malformed-input cases. Exercise an exclusion citing an accepted SUCCESSOR of the same finding, and refuse SUCCESSOR rulings of another finding or another round.\"\n+method = \"Run dual and single plan coverage, reference, graph, exclusion, review-verdict finding, dispute-ruling, SR/TC-diff, absent-registry, and malformed-input cases. Exercise an exclusion citing an accepted SUCCESSOR of the same finding, and refuse SUCCESSOR rulings of another finding or another round. Exercise two dispute rounds in one lane reusing ids, each resolving its own finding; cite round 1's verdict for round 2's same-id finding; exercise a binding without the record with a matching findings file beside it; exercise recorded files that are missing, directories, not TOML, or record no such id with a matching file beside them; exercise the pass-record route at HEAD with a dismissal bound without its record; and exercise a dispute bound through the entry point against its own round's finding.\"\n tier = \"Smoke\"\n-expected = \"A review verdict yields ordered F# clauses for its declared-severity findings only; a bracketed non-severity bullet yields no clause and does not shift later F# ids. A covered or reasoned-excluded finding passes; an uncovered finding, bad reference or plan graph, unexplained clause gap in either run (SINGLE Done-when or finding, DUAL goal clause), undeclared or reasonless exclusion, an F# exclusion citing a FIX, ESCALATE, non-accepted, mismatched, missing, unrelated, another-round same-numbered, or unshown dispute finding, an item SR no row cites (even if excluded), or a TC verifying an item SR that no row names nor excludes exits 1 by name. A mixed declared/verdict findings file, malformed input, or a missing Done-when exits 2; DUAL report bytes remain unchanged; absent registries note without failure. An accepted SUCCESSOR of the same finding resolves its exclusion; a SUCCESSOR of another finding or another round, or an unaccepted SUCCESSOR, leaves the finding unresolved; refusals of FIX or ESCALATE name DISMISS and SUCCESSOR as the resolving rulings.\"\n+expected = \"A review verdict yields ordered F# clauses for its declared-severity findings only; a bracketed non-severity bullet yields no clause and does not shift later F# ids. A covered or reasoned-excluded finding passes; an uncovered finding, bad reference or plan graph, unexplained clause gap in either run (SINGLE Done-when or finding, DUAL goal clause), undeclared or reasonless exclusion, an F# exclusion citing a FIX, ESCALATE, non-accepted, mismatched, missing, unrelated, another-round same-numbered, or unshown dispute finding, an item SR no row cites (even if excluded), or a TC verifying an item SR that no row names nor excludes exits 1 by name. A mixed declared/verdict findings file, malformed input, or a missing Done-when exits 2; DUAL report bytes remain unchanged; absent registries note without failure. An accepted SUCCESSOR of the same finding resolves its exclusion; a SUCCESSOR of another finding or another round, or an unaccepted SUCCESSOR, leaves the finding unresolved; refusals of FIX or ESCALATE name DISMISS and SUCCESSOR as the resolving rulings. Two dispute rounds in one lane reusing ids each resolve their own finding, while round 1's verdict cited for round 2's same-id finding refuses. A binding without the record refuses by name with a matching findings file beside it; recorded files that are missing, directories, not TOML, or record no such id also refuse by name with a matching file beside them. The pass-record route at HEAD refuses a dismissal bound without its record. A dispute bound through the entry point resolves only its own round's finding.\"\n automated = \"Yes\"\n-evidence = \"tests/test_plan_coverage.py::test_the_shared_step_turns_a_real_verdict_into_F_clauses; tests/test